## Design: the look is a contract too

The look of the project is one committed file, [`.ai/design/tokens.json`](.ai/design/tokens.json): colours (light and dark), spacing, radius and type sizes, by name.
Screens are built from those names and never from literal values<%#hasWeb%>; the web app reads them as CSS variables (`<%webTokensFile%>`)<%/hasWeb%><%#hasMobile%>; the mobile app reads them as Dart constants (`<%mobileTokensFile%>`)<%/hasMobile%>, both generated from the file.

- **Bring the design in**: <%#hasDesignUrl%>the design is at <%designUrl%>. <%/hasDesignUrl%>Export its variables as design tokens into `.ai/design/tokens.json` (<%designToolName%> or by hand), then `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design sync`.
- **Say what to build**: every PRD has a **UI & design** section (the frame, every state of each screen, new tokens, exact copy), with reference images in the spec's `design/` folder. The agent builds from it (skill `.ai/skills/implement-ui`).
- **Check it**: `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design check`<%#designEnforced%> is the `repo.design` gate: it fails on a hard-coded colour, space, radius or font size, on an out-of-date generated file and on a PRD without a UI & design section<%/designEnforced%>.
  It proves the screen is made of the design's values, not that it looks like the frame: a reviewer compares the PR's screenshots with the references. Details: [`.ai/design/README.md`](.ai/design/README.md).

<%/hasDesign%>
## Use it from an AI desktop app (Claude Code, Codex, ...)

You can open this folder in a desktop coding app instead of letting `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc` start an agent CLI. What changes is **who is in charge**.
`implement` is in charge of its agent: it refuses a dirty tree, installs the toolchain, runs a baseline, gives the agent a fixed prompt with fixed permissions, stops if the agent touches the spec or the gate config, then verifies and heals within a budget.
In a desktop app the agent is interactive and nothing enforces any of that. So keep the tool as the **judge** and use the app as the **agent**.

**Identical to the CLI, nothing to set up:** open the folder in the app, open the terminal built into the app, and run the commands above there (`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement FEAT-001`). The tool starts the agent itself, so the behaviour is the CLI's by construction. It needs that agent's CLI installed and signed in (`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc agent` shows which are).

**Claude Code (the Claude desktop app's Code tab, or the CLI): slash commands.** Open this folder and type `/sdlc` in the chat to pick one. The project ships one command per step (`.claude/skills/`): Claude does the work as the agent, and `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc` stays the judge. Only you can start them (Claude never runs one on its own), and nothing is ever committed.

| Type | What it does |
|---|---|
| `/sdlc-doctor` | checks that the tools this project needs are installed |
| `/sdlc-setup` | installs the dependencies |
| `/sdlc-gates` | the fast checks: lint, types, unit tests, build, contract, security |
| `/sdlc-verify` | every gate, then each acceptance criterion against the running system; it only reports |
| `/sdlc-new user-login` | creates a feature's spec and writes the PRD and checks with you in chat |
| `/sdlc-spec requirements.pdf` | drafts a spec from a requirements document |
| `/sdlc-implement FEAT-001` | builds the feature and proves it, healing until green |

The names start with `sdlc-` on purpose: Claude Code has built-in commands such as `/doctor`, and a project command with the same name would replace them.
Step 1 below is not needed for these commands. The steps 1 to 5 are what `/sdlc-implement` does for you: follow them by hand in other apps.

**For other apps, or to see what `/sdlc-implement` does,** follow these steps. Each one stands in for something `implement` does for you.

1. **Let the app read this project's rules (once).** Codex reads `AGENTS.md` by itself. Claude Code reads `CLAUDE.md`, so make that file import `AGENTS.md`, and commit it (`implement` wants a clean tree):
   ```bash
   echo '@AGENTS.md' > CLAUDE.md && git add CLAUDE.md && git commit -m "chore: let Claude Code read AGENTS.md"
   ```
   Optional, macOS / Linux / WSL: list the playbooks in `.ai/skills/` as skills of each tool. The prompt and `AGENTS.md` already name every rule and playbook by path, so an agent reads them either way. Keep these links local (second line), because a clean tree is required:
   ```bash
   for dir in .claude/skills .agents/skills; do mkdir -p "$dir"; for s in .ai/skills/*/; do ln -s "../../.ai/skills/$(basename "$s")" "$dir/$(basename "$s")"; done; done
   printf '.claude/\n.agents/\n' >> .git/info/exclude
   ```
   Check that the app really loaded the rules: start a chat and ask it *"which instructions from this repository did you load?"*. It should name `AGENTS.md` and its rules.

2. **Give it the permissions the CLI gives its agent: edit this project and run its commands, nothing more.**
   - *Claude Code:* mode **Accept edits** (called *Auto accept edits* in older versions), and let it run the tool without asking, by answering "always allow" the first time or by putting this in `.claude/settings.local.json`:
     ```json
     { "permissions": { "allow": ["Bash(./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc *)"] } }
     ```
   - *Codex:* **Ask for approval** or **Approve for me**. Both use the `workspace-write` sandbox, which is what `codex exec --sandbox workspace-write` gives the CLI agent.
   - Do not pick **Bypass permissions** or **Full access**: the CLI's agent never has them, and the integrity guard below is manual.
   - Installing dependencies needs the network: run `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc setup` and `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc sync` yourself in the app's terminal rather than widening what the agent may do.
   - Work in the project folder itself, not in a git worktree. A worktree is a fresh checkout: no `node_modules` or `.venv`, no `.ai/evidence`, and no uncommitted spec. If you use one, commit the spec first and run `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc setup` inside it.

3. **Start from the same state the CLI requires:** a clean tree, the toolchain installed, and a green baseline.
   ```bash
   git add .ai/specs && git commit -m "spec: FEAT-001"   # from here the spec is frozen: any later change shows in git
   ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc setup
   ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc gates                                   # green BEFORE the agent starts, so its failures are its own
   ```

4. **Hand the agent the exact prompt the CLI would send.**
   ```bash
   ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement FEAT-001 --dry-run --agent claude   # nothing runs; the name only has to be a preset, installed or not
   ```
   It prints `prompt: .ai/evidence/<run>/implement.prompt.md`: your PRD and checks inside the same instructions `implement` uses. In the app, open a chat on this folder and send:
   `Read .ai/evidence/<run>/implement.prompt.md and do exactly what it says.` (the Copilot and Cursor presets use this very sentence). If the agent stops with questions, they are in `QUESTIONS.md` next to that prompt: answer them in `prd.md`, commit, and start again.

5. **Let the tool judge, and heal from the app.** First the check the CLI does when the agent finishes, then the loop with *you and the app* as the agent for this one run (`--agent ""`; nothing is saved):
   ```bash
   git status --short -- .ai/specs .ai/sdlc.json scripts .github/workflows   # must print nothing: the spec and the gates are not the agent's to change
   git log --oneline -3                                                       # the agent must not have committed; you review and commit
   ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc sync                                                        # installs any dependency the agent added
   ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc loop --agent ""
   ```
   `loop` runs the gates and the facts. When something fails it writes `.ai/evidence/<run>/heal-1.prompt.md`, prints the path and waits. Send the app `Read .ai/evidence/<run>/heal-1.prompt.md and do exactly what it says.`, and when it is done press **Enter** in the terminal to verify again. Run it in the terminal yourself: a shell started by an agent cannot wait for Enter.
   It stops exactly as the CLI does: green (exit 0), <%maxIterations%> rounds, <%noProgressLimit%> identical failures in a row, or the spec or gate files changed while healing (exit 4). **"Done" is that exit code and `.ai/evidence/latest/report.md`, never the agent's own message.**

**Drafting a spec from a document works the same way:** `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc spec requirements.pdf --dry-run --agent claude` writes the prompt (it contains the document's text); send it to the app; then `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc facts --feature FEAT-001 --runtime none` (use the id the dry run prints after "would create") has the tool check that every criterion has a check. Review the draft before you implement it (`.ai/skills/review-spec`).

**What stays different.** The agent's permissions, model and effort are the app's settings, not the CLI preset's. The prompt is the same, but the agent is not headless: it may ask you things mid-run. And only step 5's two `git` lines stand in for the CLI's integrity guard and commit check, so run them every time.


